NAME
    image - draw a P8I picture, or grab the screen into one

SYNOPSIS
    image x y file
    image read x0 y0 x1 y1 file

DESCRIPTION
    Two inverse verbs, straight from the shell (no BASIC needed).

    `image x y file` draws a P8I picture with its top-left at (x,y). The
    file carries its own width, height and depth, so nothing else is
    needed; a file that is missing prints ?No file, and one that is not
    P8I, the wrong version or depth, or ends early prints ?NOT P8I.
    Pixels past any screen edge are simply discarded, so a picture may
    hang off any side (x and y may be negative).

    `image read x0 y0 x1 y1 file` is the screen GRAB: it reads every
    pixel of the rectangle (any two opposite corners, sorted for you,
    coordinates 0-479 and 0-271) and writes it as a fresh P8I file,
    REPLACING any existing file of that name. Because P8I is
    self-describing, a grab is immediately re-drawable -- by this
    command, or by BASIC's IMAGE -- and can be lifted to a host machine
    (p8xfs get, then tools/p8img.py) as a real screenshot.

    A grab reads what the panel SHOWS: if the geometry engine has been
    page-flipping (see man g3d), the command re-joins the pages first.
    Speed, measured: this build draws at ~4,400 cycles a pixel (the
    256x256 mandrill in about ten seconds; a full-screen grab about
    fifteen). The hand-assembled twin at /bina/image.bin runs ~8x
    faster -- the mandrill in under two seconds -- with identical
    behaviour: run /bina/image.bin with the same arguments.
    The drawing pen is left on the last colour handled; set
    COLOR (BASIC) or gcolor (C) before drawing your own things after.

EXAMPLES
    image 112 8 /MANDRILL.P8I          draw the demo photo
    image read 0 0 479 271 /SHOT.P8I   grab the whole screen
    image read 100 50 163 113 /Q.P8I   grab a 64x64 square
    image 200 100 /Q.P8I               ...and stamp it elsewhere

SEE ALSO
    basic (IMAGE statement), gfx, g3d, cube
